%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b360da9bf94d30246b40adae73f848a5, type: 3}
  m_Name: TutorialDatabase
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::TutorialDatabase
  tutorials:
  - id: T_SHOPPING
    triggerFlag: tutorial.shopping_available
    behaviourId: Shopping
    priority: 80
    activationPhase: Market
    completionFlags:
    - tutorial.shopping_lesson_complete
    steps:
    - stepId: shop
      text: 'Welcome to your bistro! Pick any recipe on today''s menu and buy what
        it needs: {examples}. Each food''s category must match the slot exactly;
        flip a card to see its category. Explore the stores and shelves, then drag
        packages into your cart. You may buy extras, but checkout must be affordable
        and leave you enough ingredients for at least one recipe. Open the cart to
        remove packages. Pantry and Recipes remain available at any time.'
  - id: T_SORTING
    triggerFlag: tutorial.sorting_available
    behaviourId: TextOnly
    priority: 90
    activationPhase: FoodSorting
    completionFlags:
    - tutorial.sorting_lesson_complete
    steps:
    - stepId: explain
      text: 'Before cooking, sort the new foods into Green, Yellow or Red. These
        are the game''s Traffic Light groups: Green foods are the everyday foundation
        and the only foods that build health. Yellow foods are ones customers are
        moving away from. Red foods are the ones to avoid. Flip cards to inspect
        ingredients. Each new food you sort correctly gives back {maxDiscount} of
        what you paid for it today. Foods you already know and wrong answers give
        nothing back. You can rearrange cards before submitting. There is no retry
        after submission; a wrong food can return on a later purchase.'
  - id: T_COOKING
    triggerFlag: tutorial.cooking_available
    behaviourId: FirstMeal
    priority: 70
    activationPhase: Service
    completionFlags:
    - tutorial.cooking_lesson_complete
    steps:
    - stepId: choose
      text: Let's cook the ingredients you prepared. Choose any recipe you have ingredients
        for. You can still inspect your pantry or return to the main menu.
    - stepId: build
      text: Drag one serving at a time into the recipe slots. Required slots need
        the exact category and count. Optional slots may stay empty, or be filled
        exactly. A red rim means something needs attention. Open a slot to drag a
        serving back out. Review the dish whenever you like; this first lesson requires
        a successful recipe before you can confirm serving.
    - stepId: feedback
      text: 'Your first recipe is built! You used {ingredients}. This dish changed
        health by {healthDelta} and covered these Daily Dozen groups: {dailyDozen}.
        In this game, Green servings and the Daily Dozen groups they cover contribute
        to health. Each Daily Dozen group a guest has not eaten before adds extra
        health for them; groups they have already had add nothing extra, so vary
        the plants you serve each guest. Positive health gains per dish are capped.
        Aim for variety within the recipe''s slots, rather than piling on extra servings.
        From now on, you may also serve imperfect recipes and face their consequences.'
  - id: T_CRASH
    triggerFlag: tutorial.first_crash_dialogue_completed
    behaviourId: TextOnly
    priority: 100
    activationPhase: Service
    completionFlags:
    - tutorial.rush_crash_lesson_complete
    steps:
    - stepId: explain
      text: 'You have now heard both sides of a rush and crash. A brief lift can
        feel encouraging, yet it may not last. Watch the customer''s experience over
        time. Here, rush/crash is visual and dialogue feedback: it adds no extra
        health penalty beyond the dish''s normal calculation. Think about the ingredients
        and the overall meal, rather than treating a burst of energy as proof of
        a good result.'
  - id: T_RENT
    triggerFlag: tutorial.rent_upcoming
    behaviourId: TextOnly
    priority: 95
    activationPhase: Market
    completionFlags:
    - tutorial.rent_lesson_complete
    steps:
    - stepId: explain
      text: 'A letter from the landlord: the opening grace period ends tomorrow.
        From tomorrow morning, rent of ${rent} is charged every day before the market
        opens, even on a quiet day. Your money can dip below zero, but you can only
        shop while your balance is positive, so keep a cushion. A popular bistro
        also earns walk-in takeaway each evening, so delighting guests is what keeps
        the lights on. Guests will soon bring requests too: answering them builds
        popularity, and ignoring them costs some.'
  - id: T_SELL
    triggerFlag: tutorial.money_low
    behaviourId: TextOnly
    priority: 96
    activationPhase: Market
    completionFlags:
    - tutorial.sell_lesson_complete
    steps:
    - stepId: explain
      text: 'Money is running low: tomorrow''s rent of ${rent} could push you below
        zero, and below zero the shops won''t take your order. You can raise cash
        by selling food you won''t use: open Pantry and drag servings onto SELL,
        then press Sell. Each serving sells for {sellRate} of its lowest shelf price
        (or of what you paid, if that was less). Food that would spoil before you
        can cook it is the best thing to sell.'
  - id: T_REQUESTS
    triggerFlag: okinawa_chef_arrived
    behaviourId: TextOnly
    priority: 85
    activationPhase: Service
    completionFlags:
    - tutorial.requests_lesson_complete
    steps:
    - stepId: explain
      text: From today, guests may arrive with a request such as Savory, Creamy or
        Crunchy. Each recipe on the menu lists what it is good for. Serving a recipe
        that answers a guest's request delights them and grows your popularity much
        faster; leaving a request unanswered costs some popularity. Health still
        comes only from the foods you choose. Comfort is answered by any complete
        recipe with an average ingredient appeal of at least {comfort}.
  - id: T_FARMER
    triggerFlag: tutorial.food_aid_given
    behaviourId: TextOnly
    priority: 99
    activationPhase: Service
    completionFlags:
    - tutorial.farmer_lesson_complete
    steps:
    - stepId: explain
      text: 'Your pantry can''t complete any recipe on the menu, so a local farmer
        has stepped in with a small crate of plain staples: enough for one simple
        dish. The farmer helps at most once a day, and only when nothing on the menu
        can be made. Gifted food spoils after a day and has low appeal, so guests
        won''t be thrilled. Shop, or sell food you won''t use, to get back on your
        feet.'
  - id: T_DAY
    triggerFlag: tutorial.toolbar_day
    behaviourId: TextOnly
    priority: 110
    activationPhase: Any
    completionFlags: []
    steps:
    - stepId: explain
      text: Your bistro has {totalDays} days to make its name. Each day you shop
        at the market, sort what you bought, then cook for your guests. Rent of ${rent}
        is charged every morning from day {rentStart}, and guests start bringing
        requests on day {cravingsStart}. After day {totalDays}, your popularity and
        the community's wellbeing decide how the story ends.
  - id: T_POPULARITY
    triggerFlag: tutorial.toolbar_popularity
    behaviourId: TextOnly
    priority: 110
    activationPhase: Any
    completionFlags: []
    steps:
    - stepId: explain
      text: 'Popularity is how well known your bistro is. It decides how many guests
        come each day: more seats open at {guestThresholds} popularity, up to {maxGuests}
        guests. Above {walkInThreshold}, walk-in customers also buy takeaway every
        evening, about ${walkInPer100} for each 100 points above it. Delighted guests,
        answered requests and tasty plates raise it; mismatched dishes and ignored
        requests lower it.'
  - id: T_WALLET
    triggerFlag: tutorial.toolbar_wallet
    behaviourId: TextOnly
    priority: 110
    activationPhase: Any
    completionFlags: []
    steps:
    - stepId: explain
      text: Your wallet pays for food at the market, and guests pay you, plus tips,
        for every dish you serve. From day {rentStart}, rent of ${rent} is charged
        every morning. Your money can dip below zero, but stores only take orders
        while you have money, and ending the run deep in debt closes the bistro.
        Short on cash? Sell food you won't use from the Pantry for {sellRate} of
        its price.
  fingerprint: O81ZkccRgve73aIq6SIeoHZD6LGkMm/AEhRFmd2UQfs=
